Martin Read <mpread@chiark.greenend.org.uk> writes:

> "Kent Paul Dolan" <xanthian@well.com> wrote:
>>Which merely highlights that NetHack _also_ needs to
>>be rewritten in a language that does garbage
>>collection, since C still, IIUC, fails to reuse the
>>freed space of a freed buffer without explicit
>>programmer management of the free space, making
>>use of realloc() into a memory leak.

I'm boggled.

I'm not aware of any C environment where 'realloc' doesn't free the
original memory if the allocated space moves to another region. In
fact, the C99 standard explicitly requires that the old memory is
freed. (The C88 standard doesn't tell that).

Sure, there may be some memory fragmentation issues where allocated
and freed memory sections alternate and the free parts are too small
that they can't be reused, but I find it rather unlikely in this case
since they would be used to allocate (possibly short) strings. And
anyway, short of rewriting the standard library, the programmer
doesn't have any control over this. Like a programmer can't really
control garbage collecting in Java (and numerous Java implementations
have had bugs in garbage collection leaving memory leaks). 

> The behaviour of free(), malloc(), realloc(), and calloc() is
> implementation-dependent, I suspect.

Only if you try to free or reallocate a random pointer. Which is a
programming error, by the way. 

> The problem with Java is that the JVMs are all buggy and Sun have
> just shitcanned one of the BSDs.

A couple of my friends have implemented a networked free game using
Java. It runs differently on every JVM they have tried it on. And none
of them runs it completely correct: in one a couple of buttons vanish
into thin air, in another a menu doesn't work, and so on.

Java is a "write-once-for-every-different-JVM-implementaion"
-language. With C you can at least control how your program compiles
and executes in different environments. With Java you can only pray.

To get back on topic I might add that using object-orientation could
probably make nethack more flexible and more extensible. However, you
would have to recode it from scratch. And that would be a *HUGE*
amount of work. I seem to recall that there have been at least several
previous attemps in doing the rewrite but all have dwindled down after
a week or a month.

If I had to write a roguelike I would probably do it in Objective-C
as it is much simpler than C++. Though, in the end the choice of
language is not so important as you can write OO code in almost any
language you wish. 

